title: A truncated status line or header block is still accepted silently
updated: 2026-08-28T01:14:36Z
---

karr #64 made a response body that falls short of what it announced fatal --
Content-Length, chunk data, chunk header, missing zero chunk. The head was
left out on purpose and is this ticket.

_read_head still ends on whatever _read_line hands back:

  * a status line with no terminator is parsed anyway -- 'HTTP/1.1 20' comes
    back as status '20', measured over a socketpair whose peer writes those
    11 bytes and closes.
  * the header loop is `while (my $line = $self->_read_line(...))`, so an EOF
    inside the header block ends it as though the blank line had arrived. The
    headers that did come are kept and the response is read on with them.

Neither is covered by #64's check, and correctly so: nothing in a status line
or a header block announces its own length, so there is no announcement to
compare a short one against. Detecting it needs a different rule -- "the
header block ended without its blank line" -- which is a claim about HTTP
framing rather than about a promise the response made.

Whether it is worth making fatal is the open question. A truncated head is
followed by a body read with the wrong headers, which is usually a
close-delimited read of nothing; the damage is a bogus status rather than a
half body. t/read_timeout.t asserts the current behaviour in two site_ok
subtests ('half a status line', 'the header block stops halfway'), both of
which would be replaced the way #64 replaced four others.
